The deployment of MinIO is exactly what your local cluster environment needs to satisfy Langfuse's storage architecture.

To bridge the gap between Langfuse Web and your newly created MinIO instance, you need to execute two final integration steps: create the destination bucket inside MinIO, and tell Langfuse how to connect to it.

Step 1: Create the langfuse Bucket inside MinIO
Before the web app can upload trace JSON payloads, the target bucket must explicitly exist on the MinIO server. You can provision this immediately via a quick, temporary Kubernetes job pod:

Bash
kubectl run minio-setup --rm -i --restart=Never \
  --namespace=openlit-demo \
  --image=minio/mc \
  --command -- sh -c '
    mc alias set myminio http://minio:9000 "$MINIO_ACCESS_KEY" "$MINIO_SECRET_KEY";
    mc mb myminio/langfuse;
    mc policy set public myminio/langfuse;
    echo "Bucket successfully created!";
  '
Step 2: Bind Langfuse to MinIO
Since you deployed Langfuse using Helm into the langfuse namespace, create a local override file named langfuse-storage-values.yaml containing the networking paths pointing to your openlit-demo namespace:

YAML
# langfuse-storage-values.yaml
env:
  # Instruct Langfuse to stop defaulting to AWS cloud validation rules
  LANGFUSE_STORAGE_PROVIDER: "s3"
  
  # Connection keys matching your MinIO environmental credentials
  AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: "langfuse"
  AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: "${MINIO_SECRET_KEY}"
  AWS_REGION: "us-east-1"
  
  # Core tracking references
  LANGFUSE_S3_BUCKET: "langfuse"
  
  # Cross-Namespace DNS link routing back to your MinIO Service endpoint
  LANGFUSE_S3_ENDPOINT: "http://minio.openlit-demo.svc.cluster.local:9000"
  
  # Vital override: forces traditional path style structure (http://endpoint/bucket) 
  # instead of cloud subdomains (http://bucket.endpoint)
  LANGFUSE_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE: "true"
Apply the values to your active Helm setup using an upgrade patch:

Bash
helm upgrade langfuse langfuse/langfuse \
  --namespace langfuse \
  --reuse-values \
  -f langfuse-storage-values.yaml
If you installed Langfuse manually through a standard deployment file rather than Helm, append those exact six env name/value pairs into the containers.env array of your langfuse-web deployment file and execute:

Bash
kubectl rollout restart deployment langfuse-web -n langfuse



kubectl patch deployment langfuse-web -n langfuse --type='json' -p='[
  {"op": "add", "path": "/spec/template/spec/containers/0/env", "value": [
    {"name": "LANGFUSE_STORAGE_PROVIDER", "value": "s3"},
    {"name": "AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID", "value": "langfuse"},
    {"name": "AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY", "value": "${MINIO_SECRET_KEY}"},
    {"name": "AWS_REGION", "value": "us-east-1"},
    {"name": "LANGFUSE_S3_BUCKET", "value": "langfuse"},
    {"name": "LANGFUSE_S3_ENDPOINT", "value": "http://minio.openlit-demo.svc.cluster.local:9000"},
    {"name": "LANGFUSE_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE", "value": "true"},
    {"name": "NEXT_PUBLIC_LANGFUSE_S3_BUCKET", "value": "langfuse"},
    {"name": "NEXT_PUBLIC_LANGFUSE_STORAGE_PROVIDER", "value": "s3"}
  ]}
]'







